First Study on the Reactivity of Water Vapor in a Metal–Organic Framework with Pt Nanocrystals
The absorption of water in porous solids such as silica, zeolites, and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), is of great importance in both fundamental science and practical applications.[1] In particular, research on water absorption has recently attracted considerable attention in crystalline MOFs consisting of metal ions and organic ligands.[2] Owing to their high porosity and designability, MOFs have demonstrated potential applications including dehumidification,[3] heat pump,[4] and $H_{2}O$ harvesting.[5] Furthermore, the MOF nanochannels offer a characteristic cluster structure[6] or molecular motion of $H_{2}O,O,^{[7]}$ and interesting associated functions such as proton conduction,[8] which are not observable in bulk ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ .
Although much effort has been devoted to studying the sorption properties of $H_{2}O$ in MOFs, few have reported the reactivity of $H_{2}O$ vapor in MOFs and how the confined $H_{2}O$ affects reactions remains unclear. Here, we investigated the influence of a MOF pore on the reactivity of $H_{2}O$ in the gas phase for the first time. To address the reactivity of $H_{2}O$ absorbed in MOFs, we focused on the water–gas shift (WGS) reaction $(H_{2}O+C O\rightarrow H_{2}$ $+\ C O_{2})$ ) because the activation of $H_{2}O$ is the rate-limiting step of the reaction.[9] Pt nanocrystals (NCs) are well known as one of the famous catalysts for the WGS reaction; thus, we hybridized Pt NCs with a MOF. As a MOF providing $H_{2}O$ molecules, we selected a highly thermal- and water-stable Zr(IV) MOF, UiO-66 $\mathsf{\Gamma}[Z\mathsf{r}_{6}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}({\mathsf{O H}})_{4}({\mathsf{B D C}})_{6}$ (BDC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate)] consisting of $Z r_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}$ octahedral clusters connected by BDC linkers.[10] The pore size of UiO-66 is ca. $7\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ which is larger than the size of $H_{2}O$ (kinetic diameter $=2.65\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ). In addition, UiO-66 has high water absorption ability with over $0.02\:\mathrm{mol/g}$ at $90\%$ RH and 298 K.[2a]
In the WGS reaction mechanism, it has been reported that the interface between Pt catalyst and support has a key role in the dissociative activation of $H_{2}O$ as the rate-limiting step.[11] Additionally, the concentration or flow rate of $H_{2}O$ strongly affects the WGS reaction activity. Therefore, we hypothesized that UiO66 pores have three important factors to affect the WGS reaction activity of Pt NCs: (1) a condensation effect increasing the concentration of $H_{2}O$ around Pt NCs, (2) a diffusion effect of $H_{2}O$ into Pt NCs through UiO-66, and (3) a synergetic effect at the interface between UiO-66 and Pt NCs. To investigate the contribution of these factors to the catalytic activity of Pt NCs, we designed two different composite structures of Pt NCs and UiO66: Pt on UiO-66, where Pt NCs are located on the surface of UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66, where Pt NCs are coated with UiO-66 (Figure 1a). In this work, we first demonstrate the unique behavior of the catalytic activity originating from the competitive effects of $H_{2}O$ condensation and diffusion in UiO-66 pores. Furthermore, with a thinner UiO-66 coating on Pt NCs, the catalytic activity of Pt@UiO-66 was enhanced significantly.
In a typical synthesis, Pt NCs were first prepared using polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) as a protecting agent.[12] The mean diameter of the obtained Pt NCs was estimated to be $7.9\pm1.0\mathsf{n m}$ from transmission electron microscope (TEM) images (Figure S1). The obtained Pt NCs were dispersed with the presynthesized UiO-66 in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), and the mixture was sonicated for $12\mathsf{h}$ to obtain Pt on UiO-66. For the preparation of Pt@UiO-66, the Pt NCs were mixed with $Z r C l_{4}$ , ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ , and acetic acid in DMF and the mixture was heated at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\ h$ in an oven using a solvothermal method. As a reference catalyst, $Z\mathsf{r O}_{2}$ -supported Pt NCs (Pt on $z r O_{2}$ ) was also prepared by the same method as Pt on UiO-66 (see Experimental Section and Supporting Information for synthesis details).
Figure 1b shows the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of Pt on UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66. The PXRD patterns of the composites consisted of diffraction patterns from both Pt and UiO66 lattices. The lattice constants of Pt and UiO-66 were estimated to be $3.93\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $20.76\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ for Pt on UiO-66, and $3.93\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and 20.75 Å for Pt@UiO-66, respectively, by Le Bail fitting (Figures S2–S5 and Table S1). These values are consistent with those of pristine Pt NCs (3.93 Å) and UiO-66 (20.76 Å), indicating that the structures of UiO-66 and Pt NCs were maintained after their hybridization. We also confirmed that the local structures were preserved, by extended $\mathsf{X}$ -ray absorption fine structure analysis (Figure S6). The ratios of Pt included in the composites were estimated to be 6.4 wt $\%$ and 6.7 wt $\%$ for Pt on UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66, respectively by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy measurements.
To clarify the composite states of Pt on UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66, we performed TEM measurements. Figure 1c shows that Pt NCs in Pt on UiO-66 are located on the surface of UiO-66. In contrast, Pt NCs in Pt@UiO-66 are completely coated with UiO-66 (Figure 1d). The mean diameters of Pt NCs for Pt on UiO66 and Pt@UiO-66 were estimated to be $8.0\pm1.2$ and $7.9\pm1.0$ nm, respectively, which are in good agreement with the crystal sizes calculated from PXRD patterns (8.4(2) nm for Pt on UiO-66 and 8.3(1) nm for Pt@UiO-66, Table S1). The same sizes of Pt NCs in Pt on UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66 enable a proper discussion of how Pt NCs’ location in UiO-66 impacts the WGS reaction activity of Pt NCs.We measured ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms for pristine UiO-66 and both composites at 77 K (Figure 1e). Both composites showed typical type-I sorption behavior originating from the microporosity, as is the case with the pristine UiO-66. Then, we collected $H_{2}O$ sorption isotherms at $298~\mathsf{K}$ to investigate how much water can be absorbed in the pores of UiO-66. As shown in Figure 1f, both composites can absorb $0.023\ \mathrm{mol/g}$ at a relative pressure of 0.9. The large $H_{2}O$ absorption capacities of UiO-66 are maintained after the hybridization. This large capacity is expected to enhance WGS reaction activities of Pt on UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66, compared with $\mathsf{P t}$ on $z_{r}\circ_{2}$ (Figure S8) due to the condensation effect of $H_{2}O$ in the MOF pore even under WGS reaction conditions at high temperature.
To investigate the reactivity of the absorbed $H_{2}O$ in the UiO66, we examined the WGS reaction for the composites with a fixed-bed reactor (see Experimental Section and Figures S10 for experimental details). As shown in Figure 2, the conversion of CO to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ increased with temperature for the composites and Pt on $z_{r}\circ_{2}$ . The conversion of Pt@UiO-66 was higher, showing $10.8\%$ CO conversion at $340~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , compared with that of Pt on UiO-66 $(8.1\%)$ and that of Pt on $z r O_{2}$ $(6.4\%)$ at the same reaction temperature. The pristine structures of the composites including mean diameters of Pt NCs or the crystallinities and the porosities of UiO-66 were maintained after the WGS reaction test, as characterized by PXRD, TEM, and ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption measurements (Figures S11–S17). In addition, an electronic interaction between Pt and UiO-66 was not observed (Figure S18), which could not explain the higher activities of $\mathsf{P t}$ on UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66 compared with that of $P t$ on $z_{r}\circ_{2}$ . Thus, it should be attributed to the differences in the $H_{2}O$ absorption capacity observed between the samples (Figures 1f and S8).
OH species coming from $H_{2}O$ dissociation are reported to play a critical role in the WGS activity.[9] To address the formation and reactivity of the OH species resulting from $H_{2}O$ activation, we carried out in situ IR–mass spectra (MS) measurements under a mixed gas flow of $H_{2}O$ , CO, and ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for Pt@UiO-66, Pt on UiO-66, and Pt on ZrO2 samples. The MS spectra confirmed that the WGS reaction activity follows the same order as that displayed in Figure 2, i.e., $\mathsf{P t@U i O-66>P t}$ on UiO- $66>P t$ on $z r O_{2}$ (Figure S19). In the IR spectra of the Pt@UiO-66 sample, the formation of new OH groups at 3765 and $3734\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in addition to the growth in intensity of the $3666~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ IR band, and the development of another IR band at $2080~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are observed with the in situ WGS reaction (Figure 3a). The weak IR bands at 3765 and $3734\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are attributed to Pt–OH species[9a,13] and the peak at $3666~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ corresponds to the OH-stretching vibrational mode of $Z\mathsf{r}\mathrm{-OH}$ species in the thermally activated UiO-66 structure (Figure S20).[14] In addition, the $2080~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ IR band is associated with $\mathsf{P t^{0}-C O}$ species,[15] inferring a high CO surface coverage on Pt under reaction conditions, in agreement with previous reports.[9a] Similar IR spectra are observed on the working Pt on UiO-66 and Pt on $z_{r}\circ_{2}$ samples, except for the IR band associated with Pt–OH groups, which is absent in these samples (Figure 3b, c). In the Pt on $z_{r}\circ_{2}$ sample, the new IR band at 3726 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ with the in situ WGS reaction corresponds to Zr–OH species, as observed on the pristine $Z r O_{2}$ support (Figure S21). Because Pt–OH is an active intermediate species in the WGS reaction, its presence could explain the higher WGS reactivity on the Pt@UiO66 sample under the selected operating conditions. The high interface between $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{P t}}$ NCs and UiO-66 in the Pt NCs-coated sample, and the enhanced $H_{2}O$ adsorption in the pores of UiO-66 may promote $H_{2}O$ activation on the most acidic $Z r^{4+}$ Lewis sites in proximity to Pt sites, favoring $\mathsf{P t\mathrm{-}O H}$ formation (Figure S22). In the case of Pt on UiO-66 and Pt on $z_{r}\circ_{2}$ , the interaction between $Z r^{4+}$ sites of the UiO-66 or $Z r O_{2}$ structure and Pt NCs is less, disfavoring Pt–OH formation, as certainly confirmed from the IR spectra. Besides Pt–OH, Zr–OH can also be considered as an active site in the WGS reaction. To verify it, IR–MS studies have been performed where the sample is exposed to a CO flow in the absence of $H_{2}O$ (see Experimental Section for the experimental details). $Z r\mathrm{-}O H$ groups and no $\mathsf{P t\mathrm{-}O H}$ groups are detected in the IR spectra of the samples under ${\sf N}_{2}$ flow at $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ prior to CO exposure (Figure S23). Once the flow is switched from ${\sf N}_{2}$ to CO, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and ${\sf H}_{2}$ are detected in the MS spectra corresponding to a reaction between Pt–CO and the $Z\mathsf{r}\mathrm{-OH}$ located in the interface with the Pt NCs (Figure S24). The higher reactivity of the Pt@UiO66 versus Pt on UiO-66 and Pt on $z_{r}\circ_{2}$ samples corresponds with a higher density of Pt ${\mathsf{N C-Z r}}^{4+}$ interface sites in the former one.
These IR results confirm our previous discussion that the UiO-66 coating would enhance the catalytic activity of Pt NCs, compared with Pt on UiO-66 due to the high ratio of the contact interface between Pt NCs and UiO-66. As shown in Figure 2, Pt@UiO-66 exhibited $10.9\%$ conversion at $340~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which is 1.4 times higher than Pt on UiO-66, demonstrating that UiO-66 coating caused further promotion of WGS reaction activity. However, some catalytic studies in metal NCs/MOF hybrid systems show the existence of condensation effects or diffusion effects of reactants in MOFs.[16] However, there is a lack of discussion between the effects of condensation and diffusion in the catalytic mechanism. To clarify this, we investigated the WGS reaction activity at $320^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with changing the flow rate of $H_{2}O$ from 11.4 to 114 standard cubic centimeters per minute (sccm). The flow rates of CO and Ar were constant at 10 and 50 sccm, respectively. Figure 4a shows the CO conversion of Pt@UiO-66 and Pt on UiO-66 as a function of the ratio $H_{2}O/C O$ . The conversion increased with the concentration of $H_{2}O$ according to Le Chatelier’s principle.
At a slow flow rate of $H_{2}O$ (11.4 sccm), the WGS reaction activity of Pt@UiO-66 was higher than that of Pt on UiO-66 (Figure S25). With a higher flow rate of $H_{2}O$ at 57 sccm, the activity of Pt on UiO-66 increases and was comparable to that of Pt@UiO-66 (Figure S26). Further increase in $H_{2}O$ flow rate to 114 sccm resulted in the reverse phenomenon, and the activity of Pt on UiO-66 (Figure S27) was higher than that of Pt@UiO-66. The interesting crossover behavior of catalytic activity observed in Pt on UiO-66 and Pt@UiO-66 is considered to originate from the competitive effects of $H_{2}O$ condensation and diffusion in UiO-66 pores (Figure 4b).
At a low concentration of $H_{2}O$ , the shell of UiO-66 is expected to provide the condensed $H_{2}O$ to the core of Pt NCs,[17] resulting in the higher catalytic activity of Pt@UiO-66, compared with that of Pt on UiO-66. However, the condensation effect induced by UiO-66 becomes weaker under a high concentration of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ . Instead, the diffusion of $H_{2}O$ into Pt NCs through UiO-66 is a dominant factor to determine the catalytic activity. It is reported that the diffusion of guest molecules confined in the pores of MOFs is relatively slow compared with bulk gaseous molecules.[18] Considering that the thickness of UiO-66 in Pt@UiO-66 is very large at ca $80\ \mathsf{n m}$ , the slow $H_{2}O$ diffusion in the shell of UiO-66 would suppress the activity of Pt@UiO-66. Consequently, under a high $H_{2}O$ concentration, the activity of Pt@UiO-66 becomes lower than that of Pt on UiO-66. Our results first experimentally demonstrate that both effects of condensation and diffusion of reactants should be an important consideration for the rational design of catalysts based on metal NCs/MOF hybrid.
With the important insight obtained through the comparison of catalytic activities of Pt@UiO-66 and Pt on UiO-66, we synthesized Pt@UiO-66 having a thinner shell of UiO-66 to enhance further the catalytic activity by minimizing $H_{2}O$ diffusion time in UiO-66. Figure 4c indicated that we successfully synthesized Pt NCs coated with thinner UiO-66 of ca $40\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Pt@UiO-66-40nm) with half the thickness of Pt@UiO-66 by shortening the reaction time for UiO-66 shell from 24 to 3 h (see Supporting Information for the synthesis details). The Pt@UiO66-40nm was characterized by PXRD pattern, ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $77~\mathsf{K},$ and $H_{2}O$ adsorption isotherms at $298~\mathsf{K}$ in addition to the TEM image (Figures S28–33). As shown in Figure 4a, Pt@UiO-66-40nm exhibited higher WGS reaction activity than Pt@UiO-66 with a UiO-66 thickness of $80\mathsf{n m}$ in the flow rate range of $H_{2}O$ from 11.4 to 114 sccm. In particular, the thinner coating caused the double enhancement of WGS reaction activity at the stoichiometric ratio $(H_{2}O/C O\sim1)$ . The enhanced catalytic activity is considered to originate from the minimization of $H_{2}O$ diffusion time in UiO-66 due to shortening of the diffusion pathway in the pore. Furthermore, after 5 cycles, the catalytic activity of Pt@UiO-66-40nm had no obvious change (Figure S34), indicating stability of Pt@UiO-66-40nm with the high catalytic performance (Figure S35–37). These results demonstrate that the thickness of UiO-66 enables effective control of the competitive effects of $H_{2}O$ condensation and diffusion in UiO-66 pores. Our findings also emphasize that MOFs would be one of the promising platforms to modulate condensation and diffusion of $H_{2}O$ thanks to the rational designability of the hierarchical structure on a nanoscale,[19] which is difficult to achieve not only with $Z r O_{2}$ used in this study but also with other porous materials such as activated carbons and zeolites.
In summary, we studied the reactivity of $H_{2}O$ in a MOF with Pt NCs through the WGS reaction for the first time. UiO-66 was shown to be a more effective support material for the WGS reaction than $Z\mathsf{r O}_{2}$ . In situ IR measurements suggested that the high ratio of the contact interface between Pt NCs and UiO-66 is involved in the improved WGS reactivity. In addition, the comparison of catalytic activities of Pt@UiO-66 and Pt on UiO66 revealed that the competitive effects of $H_{2}O$ condensation and diffusion in UiO-66 play important roles in the catalytic activity of Pt NCs. A thinner UiO-66 coating further enhanced the WGS reaction activity of Pt NCs due to minimizing $H_{2}O$ diffusion time in UiO-66. Our findings provide valuable insights not only for the diffusibility and reactivity of $H_{2}O$ in MOFs but also for further developments of WGS reaction catalysts. It is expected that a thinner MOF coating on one of the best catalysts for WGS reaction such as $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C e O}_{2}[20]$ and $P t-N a-S i O_{2}[21]$ would offer further improvement of the catalytic activity. We hope that this study will contribute to the development of rationally designed heterogeneous catalysts.
## Experimental Section
For the synthesis of Pt on UiO-66. Pt NCs $\left\langle0.054\mathrm{mmol}\right\rangle_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ dispersed in DMF $(15\mathsf{m L})$ were sonicated with pristine UiO-66 $(150\mathrm{mg})$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ .  The dark gray suspensions thus produced were collected and washed with DMF (3 $\times30\mathrm{mL},$ and MeOH $(3\times30~\mathrm{mL}$ ) using a centrifuge. The white powder was then immersed in MeOH (30 mL) for $24~\mathsf{h}$ The washing process with MeOH was performed three times and finally immersed in acetone $(30\mathsf{m L})$ for $24\ h$ . After the washing process, the samples were activated by removing the solvent under vacuum for $15\mathrm{~h~}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The ratio of Pt included in the composite was estimated to be 6.4 wt $\%$ . For the synthesis of $\mathsf{P t@U i O-66.~Z r C l_{4}}$ (175 mg, 0.75 mmol), ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ (125 mg, 0.75 mmol), and acetic acid $(3\mathsf{m L})$ were mixed in DMF $(10\mathsf{m L})$ . Then, Pt NCs (0.075 mmol) dispersed in DMF $(40~\mathsf{m L})$ was added and kept at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathsf{h}$ The dark gray suspensions were washed and activated using the procedure described in the synthesis of Pt on UiO-66. The ratio of Pt included in the composite was estimated to be 6.7 wt $\%$ . The catalytic activities of the water–gas shift (WGS) reaction for the prepared composites were evaluated in a fixed-bed flow reactor (BEL-REA, Microtrac BEL Corp.). The inner diameter of the tubular stainless-steel reactor was $7\mathsf{m m}$ . The composites including $5\mathsf{m g}$ of Pt were placed in the middle of the reactor tube and fixed with glass wool. Then, the catalytic tests were performed with a gas mixture of $H_{2}O$ (11.4, 57.0, or 114 sccm), CO (10 sccm) and Ar (50 sccm). Deionized $H_{2}O$ was degassed using a degassing device (Gastorr AG-32-01; FLOM Co., Japan) and evaporated at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The flow rate of $H_{2}O$ vapor (11.4, 57.0, or 114 sccm) was controlled by changing the pumping rate of $H_{2}O$ liquid (0.01, 0.05, or 0.10 mL/min) with a dual pump (KP-21-01; FLOM Co., Japan). The flow rate of CO and Ar was regulated by a mass controller (5850E; Brooks Instrument, USA). The gas mixture of $H_{2}O$ , CO and Ar was preheated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a thermostatic bath and introduced in the reactor tube. The reaction temperature was increased by $20^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in the range of 140 to $340^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with a temperature control device (SDC15; Azbil Co., Japan). The heating rate was $2\ {^{\circ}\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{m i n}}$ , and the reaction time was 85 min at every reaction temperature. The reaction pressure was controlled at 0.65 MPa by a back pressure regulating valve. The reactant of CO and products such as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ were analyzed using gas chromatography with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD) (GC-2014; Shimazu Co., Japan). Activated carbon was used as the TCD column. CO conversion was calculated according to the equation: CO Conversion $(\%)=(F_{\mathsf{C O i n}}-F_{\mathsf{C O o u t}})/F_{\mathsf{C O i n}}\times$ 100 where $F_{\mathsf{C O i n}}$ and $F_{\mathsf{C O\ o u t}}$ are the inlet and outlet volumes of CO in the reactor, respectively. In situ IR spectra were recorded with a Bruker spectrometer, Vertex 70, using a DTGS detector and acquiring at $4\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ resolution. A homemade IR cell allowing in situ treatments in controlled atmospheres and temperatures from $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ was used. For the IR study, the samples were pressed into self-supported wafers and treated in a vacuum ( $10^{-4}$ mbar) for $1.5\mathsf{h}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Afterward, the temperature was increased to reaction temperature (i.e., $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ) under ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ flow at 20 mL/min. In the in situ WGS studies, the sample was exposed to a flow of $H_{2}O/C\mathsf{O}/\mathsf{N}_{2}(4.5/0.4/15.1\mathsf{s c c m})$ operating at $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The reaction products were analyzed by online mass spectrometry running in the multi-ion mode using a Balzer (QMG 220M1; Omnistar) spectrometer. The selected $m/z$ values used in the identification of each compound were 28 (CO), 44 $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ 18 $(H_{2}O)$ , and 2 (H2). In another set of experiments, the sample once in ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ flow at $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ was exposed to a ${\mathsf{C O}}/{\mathsf{N}}_{2}$ (0.4/19.6 sccm) flow. Spectra have been acquired at each step of the experimental setup.
